WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:06.380 align:start position:0%
 
søren kierkegaard  was a brilliant gloomy anxiety written  often hilarious Danish 19th century  philosopher the author of 22 books of  which 3 continued to make his name he  was born in an immensely wealthy family  in Copenhagen in 1813 the youngest of  seven children death was around him  constantly from a young age and was to  obsess him throughout his career it is  in a sense his only theme not only was  he extremely physically frail by the  time he was twenty-two all his siblings  had died except for he and a brother it  drove him to furious production of books  of the 15 years on a single day in 1843  he published no less than three works he  wasn't writing for the money he was  working to save himself and he thought  humanity as it happened he made it to  the age of 42 then died of an  excruciatingly spinal disease in  either/or and fear and trembling what  kick yard wants us to do above all is  wake up and give up our cozy sentimental  illusions he systematically attacks the  pillars of modern life our faith in  family our trust in work our attachment  to love and our general sense that life  has purpose and meaning his enemies with  a smug in all their guises particularly  the prosperous Danish ought bourgeoisie  and the members of the established  Danish Church he tells us as I grew up I  opened my eyes and saw the real world  and I began to laugh and I haven't  stopped since I saw that the meaning of  life was to get a livelihood that the  goal of life was to be a high court  judge that the brightest joy of love was  to marry a well-off girl that wisdom was  what the majority said it was that  passion was to give a speech that  courage was to risk being fined $10 that  cordiality was to say you're welcome  after a meal and that the fear of God  was to go to communion once a year  that's what I saw and I laughed kick  guard was especially caustic about the  nineteenth-century understanding of love  and the new ideology of passionate  marriage which aimed to unite desire  with prudence and suggested that one  could enjoy all the thrills of a love  affair  and at the same time all the stability  of a long-term relationship but kick  guard mocked the notion the one could  ever fuse romantic love with marriage  that one could have passion and sex and  at the same time children's stability  and routine he respected both he just  couldn't believe that you could have  them both at the same time in a cozy  marriage sanctified by the state and the  neighbors his belief arose out of his  own tortured love life he fell in love  with a beautiful precocious and talented  eighteen-year-old girl called Regina  Olsen only then to break off the  engagement as he realized that a try and  live with her forever would also mean  killing the love that had drawn him to  her everywhere he turned Kierkegaard saw  intolerable incompatibilities and  impossible choices it led him to one  memorable explosion in either or Mary  and you will regret it don't marry you  will also regret it marry or don't marry  you will regret it either way laugh at  the world's foolishness you will regret  it  weep over it you'll regret that to hang  yourself you'll regret it don't hang  yourself and you'll regret that -  whether you hang yourself or don't hang  yourself you will regret both this  gentleman is the essence of all  philosophy the mention of laughter is  not a coincidence Keter Kierkegaard's  philosophy is that the only intelligent  tactical response to life's horror is to  laugh defiantly at it rarely as a  philosopher taken humor as seriously  kick a guard is often described as the  founder of the philosophical movement  known as existentialism because in him  we find all the themes that would so  interest later thinkers like sarch  Camus and Heidegger the book that  fascinated the existentialist was kick  guards the concept of anxiety published  in 1844 in which he emphasized a new  word honest or angst as we know it in  English a condition where we understand  how many choices we face and how little  understanding we can ever have of how to  exercise these choices wisely ask a  keyguard wrote life can only be  understood backwards but must be lived  forwards a constant angst means that  unhappiness is more or less written into  the script of life as he wrote anyone  who has given the matter any serious  thought will know that I'm right when I  say it's not possible for anyone to be  absolutely and in every conceivable way  completely content not even for a single  half-hour of his life no one has come  into the world without crying no one  asks when you want to enter the world no  one asks when you want to leave how  empty and meaningless life is we bury a  person throw three shovels of Earth over  him drive out in a coach drive back in a  coach and console ourselves that we  still have life enough left to live but  really how long is threescore and ten  why not just get it over with  straightaway for kick guard there was  however one answer that he put forward  evermore stridently in his later works  Jesus Christ  kicki guard loathed the christianity of  the established danish church but he  adored the simple truths of the gospels  that his father had taught him as a boy  for him christianity was a religion of  extreme surrender to a theology of  almost peasant like simplicity one was  to be ready to die for Christ to give up  all attachment to worldly things and to  love all humans like one's siblings  Kierkegaard wasn't interested in  justifying his attachment to  Christianity through rational means  instead he recommended a dramatic and  now famous leap of faith where anyone  wouldn't apply one's puny mind to  attempting to prove the existence of God  one would merely switch off one's faulty  rational faculties and jump into the  idea of God as the total solution as he  put it to have faith is to lose your  mind and to win God like Marxist  communism keke guards solutions to the  problems of being human are far less  convincing and interesting than the  diagnosis of our ills few of us now make  that leap but keiki God deserves our  attention for the beautifully bitter  caustic look he cast on the human  condition he's one of the few  philosophers one can turn to when the  world has badly let us down and we're in  need of a friend who can fully  understand the dark places we're in once  the sentimental illusions that normally  keep us going fall away  you

